ROS (Robot Operating System) is a middleware software framework that allows robots to communicate and coordinate with each other. A ROS node is a software component that runs on a robot and communicates with other nodes in the ROS system. Here is an example of a simple ROS node in C++:

```cpp
#include <ros/ros.h>

// Define a custom message type
class MyMessage
{
public:
    // Constructor
    MyMessage() {}

    // Definition of the message fields
    float data;
};

// Define a callback function to handle messages
void handle_message(const MyMessage& msg)
{
    // Do something with the message data
    ROS_INFO("Received message: data = [%f]", msg.data);
}

int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
    // Initialize ROS node
    ros::init(argc, argv, "my_node");

    // Publish a message
    ros::Publisher pub = ros::Publisher("my_topic", MyMessage, MyMessage::class);
    pub.publish(MyMessage());

    // Subscribe to a topic
    ros::Subscriber sub = ros::Subscriber("my_topic", MyMessage, MyMessage::class);
    sub.connect();
    sub.waitForMessage();
    MyMessage msg;
    sub.parseMessage(msg);
    ROS_INFO("Received message: data = [%f]", msg.data);

    // Clean up ROS node
    ros::shutdown();

    return 0;
}
```

This code sets up a ROS node that publishes a message on a topic and subscribes to another topic to receive messages. The custom message type `MyMessage` defines the structure of the message data, and the `handle_message` function is a callback function that is called when a message is received.

