Automatic differentiation is a technique for generating the derivative of a function with respect to its input parameters. Here is a simple example of how it can be implemented in C#:

```
using System;

namespace AutomaticDifferentiationExample
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Define the function to be differentiated
            Func<double[], double> f = (x) => x[0] * x[1] * x[2];

            // Create an array of input values
            double[] xValues = new double[] { 1, 2, 3 };

            // Differentiate the function with respect to each input parameter
            double[] gradients = new double[xValues.Length];
            for (int i = 0; i < xValues.Length; i++)
            {
                // Differentiate the function with respect to the i-th input parameter
                gradients[i] = f(xValues.Clone()[i]);
            }

            // Print the gradients
            Console.WriteLine("Gradients:");
            for (int i = 0; i < gradients.Length; i++)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"{i}: {gradients[i]}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we define a function `f` that takes an array of double values as input and returns the product of the first, second, and third elements of the array. We then create an array of input values and differentiate the function with respect to each input parameter using the function `f`. We store the gradients in an array `gradients` and print them out.

